WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.513
Start with a simple social-feed model: users,
follow relationships and posts. Publishing a

00:00:05.513 --> 00:00:10.720
post inserts one record associated with its
author. To read a viewer's feed, find the

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.744
authors that viewer follows, retrieve their
recent posts, join any required author

00:00:15.744 --> 00:00:17.765
information and order the result.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.157
The write path is compact because each post has
one authoritative representation. The read

00:00:23.157 --> 00:00:28.085
path reconstructs a personalized view from
several sets of records. This is a sensible

00:00:28.085 --> 00:00:33.127
starting point, especially at modest scale. The
problem is not that relational databases

00:00:33.127 --> 00:00:37.825
cannot store social data; it is that a heavily
used personalized query can require

00:00:37.825 --> 00:00:41.836
increasingly expensive access to scattered data
as the workload grows.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.882
Follow one reader who subscribes to three
authors. Their posts are mixed with many other

00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:51.763
authors' posts in storage and may live on
different shards. Indexes help locate relevant

00:00:51.763 --> 00:00:56.035
rows, but they do not make every reader's
selected posts physically adjacent.

00:00:56.035 --> 00:01:01.175
A feed query can require multiple lookups,
remote requests and a merge before returning a

00:01:01.175 --> 00:01:06.027
small page. Storage systems generally benefit
from useful locality, while many small

00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:11.340
scattered accesses add work and latency. This
example should branch from the reader's follow

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.268
list to several author histories and then
converge at a sorted feed.

00:01:15.268 --> 00:01:19.998
This makes the read amplification visible
instead of attributing the delay vaguely to a

00:01:19.998 --> 00:01:20.270
join.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:25.435
Prebuilding feeds moves the join's work earlier.
When an author publishes, the system finds

00:01:25.435 --> 00:01:30.259
the followers and inserts a reference or
suitable representation into each follower's

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:35.367
materialized feed. A later feed read can use the
viewer's identifier to retrieve a compact

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:36.275
prepared result.

00:01:36.275 --> 00:01:41.280
Partitioning feeds by viewer makes the common
read involve a bounded storage destination

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.455
rather than a growing collection of author
shards. The benefit is concrete: the reader does

00:01:46.455 --> 00:01:51.687
less assembly while waiting. The cost is equally
concrete: one publication creates many feed

00:01:51.687 --> 00:01:54.304
updates, and the system stores derived copies.

00:01:54.304 --> 00:01:59.246
A post with a thousand followers can create
roughly a thousand insertions rather than one.

00:01:59.304 --> 00:02:04.536
Do not make the publishing request wait for
every follower update. First durably record the

00:02:04.536 --> 00:02:09.538
post and the work that must be performed, then
let background workers update feeds. The

00:02:09.538 --> 00:02:13.448
client can receive an accepted response while
propagation continues.

00:02:13.448 --> 00:02:18.284
This reduces the publisher's waiting time but
does not reduce total fan-out work or

00:02:18.284 --> 00:02:23.469
guarantee that every follower sees the post
immediately. In the diagram, separate durable

00:02:23.469 --> 00:02:28.771
acceptance from background completion. A queue
absorbs temporary imbalance between incoming

00:02:28.771 --> 00:02:33.957
publications and worker capacity, but sustained
overload makes backlog and delivery delay

00:02:33.957 --> 00:02:34.248
grow.

00:02:34.448 --> 00:02:39.638
Monitor the age of pending work as well as queue
length, because a small number of huge fan-

00:02:39.638 --> 00:02:41.894
out jobs can still produce a long delay.
